WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.319
Welcome to our independent study lesson on integer and mixed integer linear programming.

00:00:06.319 --> 00:00:10.484
In the previous lesson, decisions could vary continuously.

00:00:10.484 --> 00:00:15.007
Today we add decisions that must be whole numbers or yes or no.

00:00:15.007 --> 00:00:18.310
Keep paper and the Python notebook beside you.

00:00:18.310 --> 00:00:20.752
Pause whenever a question appears.

00:00:20.752 --> 00:00:26.496
The aim is to build a model, solve it, and explain why its decisions make sense.

00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:33.899
The videos are shorter than the scheduled class because your working time matters.

00:00:33.899 --> 00:00:40.023
Use fifteen minutes to recall L P, thirty for integer geometry, and thirty five for modeling.

00:00:40.023 --> 00:00:41.603
Take a ten minute break.

00:00:41.603 --> 00:00:48.516
Then spend twenty five minutes on branch and bound, forty five on the lab, and twenty on the exit ticket.

00:00:48.516 --> 00:00:53.652
Pause the video rather than trying to finish every activity at playback speed.

00:00:55.700 --> 00:00:57.830
Recall our maximization L P.

00:00:57.830 --> 00:01:02.091
A feasible production plan gives a value we can achieve.

00:01:02.091 --> 00:01:05.210
Feasible dual prices give an upper bound.

00:01:05.210 --> 00:01:08.938
When the two values agree, we have a certificate.

00:01:08.938 --> 00:01:16.775
Integer programming retains the idea of bounds, but an L P solution may no longer be an allowable plan.

00:01:16.775 --> 00:01:21.644
That distinction will drive the search procedure in this lesson.

00:01:23.700 --> 00:01:27.805
Choose the variable domain from the meaning of the decision.

00:01:27.805 --> 00:01:33.004
Tonnes can usually be fractional, while an indivisible machine count cannot.

00:01:33.004 --> 00:01:37.725
A binary variable represents whether a line is available or selected.

00:01:37.725 --> 00:01:41.145
A mixed integer model may contain all three types.

00:01:41.145 --> 00:01:50.244
Making every variable integer is unnecessary and can change the engineering problem, especially when production is a continuous flow.

00:01:52.300 --> 00:01:58.967
We first use a small illustrative batch problem, with objective units chosen only for teaching.

00:01:58.967 --> 00:02:03.038
Each batch of x earns three and each batch of y earns two.

00:02:03.038 --> 00:02:06.757
Two shared resources give the displayed inequalities.

00:02:06.757 --> 00:02:08.933
The coefficients remain linear.

00:02:08.933 --> 00:02:15.109
What changes relative to L P is the requirement that both batch counts be whole numbers.

00:02:15.109 --> 00:02:20.092
Sketch the continuous feasible region before adding the integer points.

00:02:22.100 --> 00:02:25.352
Temporarily remove the integer restriction.

00:02:25.352 --> 00:02:31.025
Subtracting the first active equation from the second gives x equal to two.

00:02:31.025 --> 00:02:34.429
Substitution gives y equal to two point five.

00:02:34.429 --> 00:02:35.941
The value is eleven.

00:02:35.941 --> 00:02:42.068
This is a useful bound, but half a batch is not permitted in the integer problem.

00:02:42.068 --> 00:02:47.060
Do not report this relaxed point as the final production decision.

00:02:49.100 --> 00:02:52.504
Rounding y upward produces the plan two, three.

00:02:52.504 --> 00:02:57.068
Its resource use is five and seven, so it violates both limits.

00:02:57.068 --> 00:03:01.776
Rounding downward gives two, two and is feasible in this example.

00:03:01.776 --> 00:03:05.543
That success does not establish a general algorithm.

00:03:05.543 --> 00:03:11.699
Other problems can have infeasible rounded points or better integer points elsewhere.

00:03:11.699 --> 00:03:17.132
We need a method that verifies feasibility and provides a bound on quality.

00:03:19.200 --> 00:03:23.771
For a problem this small, we can list all feasible integer points.

00:03:23.771 --> 00:03:29.243
The point two, two gives ten, and no other point in the finite list gives more.

00:03:29.243 --> 00:03:33.190
This independent enumeration will later check our solver.

00:03:33.190 --> 00:03:39.147
The difference between eleven and ten is the absolute relaxation gap for this example.

00:03:39.147 --> 00:03:44.064
It is not the solver stopping gap after the optimum has been certified.

00:03:46.100 --> 00:03:49.405
Removing restrictions enlarges the feasible set.

00:03:49.405 --> 00:03:56.634
Therefore a relaxed maximization can only improve or preserve the best value; it supplies an upper bound.

00:03:56.634 --> 00:03:59.800
A relaxed minimization supplies a lower bound.

00:03:59.800 --> 00:04:03.312
A feasible integer plan provides the opposite side.

00:04:03.312 --> 00:04:14.396
Before interpreting any solver bound, say whether your mathematical model is a maximization or a minimization, especially when you negated the objective in code.

00:04:16.400 --> 00:04:20.255
Pause here and test the proposed integer plan three, one.

00:04:20.255 --> 00:04:23.502
Check both inequalities before comparing profit.

00:04:23.502 --> 00:04:30.265
The first resource use is four, which passes, but the second is seven, which exceeds six point five.

00:04:30.265 --> 00:04:35.744
Its objective is eleven, but that number is not attainable under our constraints.

00:04:35.744 --> 00:04:43.184
When you resume, keep this rule: domain checks and every resource check come before interpreting an objective.
